### Summary

The diagnostics screen always reports `discovered 0`, even while a peer is connected, because `NearbyTransport.discoveredEndpoints()` reads a set that is never populated. Lost endpoints are also never removed from the discovery bookkeeping.

### Problem

Discovery reports every endpoint through `bindings.markDiscovered(endpointId)`:

```java
public void onEndpointFound(String endpointId, DiscoveredEndpointInfo info) {
    bindings.markDiscovered(endpointId);
    requestConnection(endpointId);
}
```

(`apps/android/app/src/main/java/dev/resqnet/android/transport/NearbyTransport.java:305-309`)

`PeerBindings` maintains its own discovered set (`PeerBindings.java:41`, `:123-131`), but `NearbyTransport` also declares an independent set:

```java
/** Endpoint ids discovery has reported, connected or not. */
private final java.util.Set<String> discovered =
        java.util.Collections.newSetFromMap(new java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap<>());
```

(`NearbyTransport.java:132-134`)

Nothing ever adds to that set. The public accessor reads it:

```java
public java.util.List<String> discoveredEndpoints() {
    java.util.List<String> found = new java.util.ArrayList<>(discovered);
    ...
}
```

(`NearbyTransport.java:498-502`)

`DiagnosticsActivity.describePeers()` consumes that accessor (`DiagnosticsActivity.java:127-137`), so the count is always zero and the list is always empty. The screen exists specifically to distinguish "discovered but not connected" from "never discovered"; both currently look identical.

Additionally, `onEndpointLost` calls `unbind(endpointId)` (`NearbyTransport.java:312-314`), and `PeerBindings.unbind` does not remove the endpoint from its discovered set. Delegating to `PeerBindings` without fixing this would leave lost endpoints on screen forever.

